  { number := 64, text := "## 4. Normative language" },
  { number := 65, text := "" },
  { number := 66, text := "### 4.1 RFC 2119 requirement keywords" },
  { number := 67, text := "" },
  { number := 68, text := "1. Specs SHALL use the requirement keywords defined in RFC 2119 in the RFC 2119 sense: MUST, MUST NOT, REQUIRED, SHALL, SHALL NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, RECOMMENDED, MAY, and OPTIONAL." },
  { number := 69, text := "2. Specs SHALL cite RFC 2119 (and the local `ref/` copy when present) in Normative references or an equivalent early section when they use those keywords." },
  { number := 70, text := "3. Keywords are not case sensitive under RFC 2119; this repository RECOMMENDS writing them in uppercase in normative clauses so readers can scan for requirements." },
  { number := 71, text := "4. Specs SHALL NOT invent a private mapping that weakens or redefines these keywords (for example, telling authors not to use MUST when RFC 2119 MUST is intended)." },
  { number := 72, text := "" },
  { number := 73, text := "### 4.2 Normative versus informative text" },
  { number := 74, text := "" },
  { number := 75, text := "1. Background, history, design reasoning, examples, notes, acknowledgments, and changelog narrative are informative. They explain context; they are not requirements by themselves." },
  { number := 76, text := "2. Requirements use the RFC 2119 keywords (MUST, SHALL, SHOULD, MAY, and the rest listed in Clause 4.1) in the RFC 2119 sense. A sentence that states a requirement SHALL use those keywords so readers can tell it is normative." },
  { number := 77, text := "3. Informative material SHOULD be titled or opened so a reader can tell it is not a requirements section. Examples of clear titles: \"Algorithm selection reasoning\", \"Background (informative)\", or an opening sentence that the subsection is informative." },
  { number := 78, text := "4. Authors SHALL NOT invent private jargon that redefines requirement levels or invents a parallel vocabulary for rules that sound like MUST. Use ordinary American English plus RFC 2119 keywords for requirements." },
  { number := 79, text := "5. If a requirement sentence appears inside an otherwise informative section, the RFC 2119 keyword still makes that sentence normative. Prefer keeping requirements in clearly normative sections and leaving informative sections free of mandatory rules." },
  { number := 80, text := "6. Open questions, behavior the implementation defines, and undefined behavior SHALL be labeled as such and SHALL NOT be mixed unmarked into mandatory requirements." },
