  { number := 128, text := "### 5.4 Lists" },
  { number := 129, text := "" },
  { number := 130, text := "1. Outside fenced code blocks, specs SHALL NOT use markdown bullet list markers (`-`, `*`, or `+` at the start of a list item)." },
  { number := 131, text := "2. Specs SHALL use numbered lists (`1.`, `2.`, ...) or lettered lists (`a.`, `b.`, ... / `A.`, `B.`, ...) for enumerations in prose." },
  { number := 132, text := "3. Table rows and markdown headings are not list markers and are allowed." },
  { number := 133, text := "" },
  { number := 134, text := "## 6. Character set and writing system" },
  { number := 135, text := "" },
  { number := 136, text := "### 6.1 Pure ASCII" },
  { number := 137, text := "" },
  { number := 138, text := "1. The entire specification file SHALL consist only of ASCII bytes." },
  { number := 139, text := "2. Allowed bytes are:" },
  { number := 140, text := "	a. Printable ASCII in the range 0x20 through 0x7E." },
  { number := 141, text := "	b. Horizontal tab 0x09." },
  { number := 142, text := "	c. Line feed 0x0A." },
  { number := 143, text := "3. Carriage return 0x0D SHALL NOT appear. Files SHALL use line endings that use LF and never CR." },
  { number := 144, text := "4. Other control characters (including NUL) SHALL NOT appear." },
  { number := 145, text := "5. Unicode punctuation SHALL NOT appear. Forbidden examples include em dashes, en dashes used as punctuation, smart quotation marks, and the Unicode ellipsis character. Use ASCII `-`, straight quotes, and `...` (three periods) instead." },
  { number := 146, text := "" },
  { number := 147, text := "### 6.2 American English" },
  { number := 148, text := "" },
  { number := 149, text := "1. Prose SHALL use American English spelling and wording (en_US)." },
  { number := 150, text := "2. On first use of an acronym that the expected audience might not know, specs SHOULD expand the acronym and MAY give the short form in parentheses." },
  { number := 151, text := "3. Proper nouns and stable external identifiers SHALL keep their upstream spelling and capitalization (for example ECMA-48, Floyd-Steinberg, SemVer)." },
  { number := 152, text := "" },
  { number := 153, text := "### 6.3 Spelling checkability" },
  { number := 154, text := "" },
  { number := 155, text := "Documents in scope SHALL remain checkable by the repository validator for spelling, character set, list form, hyphenation, and whitespace rules defined here. Domain terms that are correct but absent from a stock en_US dictionary MAY be listed in the repository spelling word list so that validation stays green without weakening the American English rule." },
  { number := 156, text := "" },
  { number := 157, text := "### 6.4 Line wrapping (not wrapped to a column)" },
  { number := 158, text := "" },
  { number := 159, text := "1. Spec prose SHALL NOT be wrapped to a column. Readers configure soft word wrap in their editor or viewer." },
  { number := 160, text := "2. Prefer one physical line per paragraph and per list item body." },
  { number := 161, text := "3. Deliberate line breaks remain for structure: blank lines, headings, list items (one item per line), tables, fenced code blocks, and blockquotes as blocks. The item text of a list entry SHOULD NOT wrap in the middle of a sentence onto a following physical line unless structure requires a nested block." },
  { number := 162, text := "4. The Lean checker enforces a heuristic for wrapping to a column on numbered specifications outside fenced code and tables: two consecutive body lines that are not empty where the first does not end a sentence or closed structure (does not end with `.`, `!`, `?`, or `:`) and the second continues with a lowercase letter. Full sentences that end with `.` on short lines still pass. Fenced code and table rows are exempt." },
  { number := 163, text := "5. Informative author tips (not a change to the algorithm in item 4): a common trip is a paragraph split in the middle of a sentence so the next physical line starts with a lowercase letter. Fix by joining that paragraph into one physical line (soft wrap in the editor). A short full sentence that ends with `.` on its own line, then a new sentence starting with a capital, is fine. Nested list structure and blank line paragraph breaks are not wraps to a column. Human oriented examples also appear in `README.md` under Validation." },
  { number := 164, text := "" },
  { number := 165, text := "### 6.5 Indentation (tabs)" },
  { number := 166, text := "" },
  { number := 167, text := "1. Leading indentation (whitespace from the start of a line before content) SHALL use tab characters (0x09) only, never space (0x20)." },
  { number := 168, text := "2. Spaces MAY appear in the middle of a line in prose and inside table rows (for example `| foo | bar |` cell padding). Those spaces are content or table formatting, not leading indentation." },
  { number := 169, text := "3. Nested markdown lists and other nested blocks SHALL indent with tabs (one tab per nesting level is the usual form)." },
  { number := 170, text := "4. Empty lines may be empty. Trailing whitespace is forbidden separately (Clause 6.6)." },
  { number := 171, text := "5. Language and format exceptions for source files in this repository that are not specifications: Lean sources MUST use spaces because the Lean 4 parser rejects tab indentation. YAML workflow files use spaces because common YAML tooling rejects tab indentation. Those exceptions apply to tooling files checked by the pure Nix whitespace check across the project; numbered specification markdown remains under the tab rule." },
  { number := 172, text := "" },
  { number := 173, text := "### 6.6 Trailing whitespace" },
  { number := 174, text := "" },
  { number := 175, text := "1. Lines SHALL NOT contain trailing space or tab characters before the line feed." },
  { number := 176, text := "2. The Lean checker enforces this on numbered specifications. The pure Nix flake check enforces it on text files owned by the project (see Clause 17 and `README.md`)." },
  { number := 177, text := "" },
  { number := 178, text := "## 7. Hyphenation and identifiers in prose" },
  { number := 179, text := "" },
  { number := 180, text := "1. Prefer ordinary English over hyphenated compounds. People who are not writing legal captions rarely hyphenate. Do not write modifiers such as `high-assurance`, `best-effort`, `self-improving`, `lock-free`, or `safety-critical`. Do not \"fix\" them by deleting the hyphen and leaving the same two words (`lock free`, `non markdown`, `must like`, `LF only`). Rephrase so a person would actually say it. `high assurance` may stay when it already reads as English. `3 bit`, `8 bit`, `24 bit`, and `high contrast` may stay. Say `tooling that is not markdown` rather than `non markdown tooling`. Say `tries that do not use locks` rather than `lock free tries`. Say `bootstrapped Slake that fully typechecks` rather than `fully type checking Slake`. The Lean `spec_check` leftover unstack list in `0005_CATE.md` is a floor for those leftover pairs; authors still SHALL rephrase other leftover pairs the checker does not name." },
  { number := 181, text := "2. A lowercase token with two or more hyphens (three or more segments), for example `foo-bar-baz`, SHALL NOT appear in prose outside fenced code blocks and inline code spans. That mechanical ban is a floor. It does not license a single hyphen in prose when a rephrased form works, and it does not license deleting the hyphen and leaving the same two words." },
  { number := 182, text := "3. Keep a hyphen in prose only when the token is a filename, a URL, a code identifier, or an official designation that is spelled with a hyphen (for example ITU-T, Floyd-Steinberg, ECMA-48, ISO/IEC). The mechanical checker still allows a single hyphen; authors still SHOULD use spaces." },
  { number := 183, text := "4. Proper names and stable external identifiers MAY keep their canonical hyphens (for example Floyd-Steinberg, ECMA-48, ISO/IEC, ITU-T)." },
  { number := 184, text := "5. Filenames, URLs, and code identifiers inside inline code or fenced blocks are outside the prose hyphenation ban. The tool name `pre-commit` MAY stay as the tool name." },
  { number := 185, text := "" },
  { number := 186, text := "## 8. Precision" },
  { number := 187, text := "" },
  { number := 188, text := "1. Terms with special meaning SHALL be defined before use or collected in a Definitions section." },
  { number := 189, text := "2. Normative facts SHALL prefer exact values, ranges, and enumerations over vague language (\"about\", \"roughly\", \"and so on\") when implementers need a definite answer." },
  { number := 190, text := "3. Behavior that the implementation defines, undefined behavior, or deliberately open behavior SHALL be called out with those words or in a clearly titled Open questions section." },
  { number := 191, text := "4. Specs SHALL NOT leave silent gaps on values, units, or states that a conforming implementer needs in order to interoperate." },
  { number := 192, text := "" },
  { number := 193, text := "## 9. Units and quantities" },
  { number := 194, text := "" },
  { number := 195, text := "1. Bit and byte widths in prose SHOULD be written with a space between the number and the unit word when used as adjectives or measures: `3 bit`, `8 bit`, `24 bit`. Do not treat this as a ban on `3-bit` in external quotations inside code fences." },
  { number := 196, text := "2. Prefer unambiguous numeric dates as in Clause 5.2." },
  { number := 197, text := "3. Use clear units for physical or logical quantities. Do not switch unit systems silently inside one spec." },
  { number := 198, text := "" },
  { number := 199, text := "## 10. Tables, code, and figures" },
  { number := 200, text := "" },
  { number := 201, text := "1. Fixed enumerations of values (palettes, code points, parameter maps) SHOULD be given as markdown tables." },
  { number := 202, text := "2. Fenced code blocks MAY present algorithms, examples, and ASCII diagrams." },
  { number := 203, text := "3. Specs SHALL NOT embed binary image assets. External links to figures are discouraged in core normative clauses; if used, the normative content SHALL still be understandable from the text and tables alone." },
  { number := 204, text := "4. ASCII diagrams inside fenced blocks MAY be used sparingly when they clarify structure." },
  { number := 205, text := "" },
  { number := 206, text := "## 11. File naming, numbering, and citation" },
  { number := 207, text := "" },
  { number := 208, text := "### 11.1 File names and location" },
  { number := 209, text := "" },
  { number := 210, text := "1. Specification files SHALL be named `NNNN_descriptive_name.md`." },
  { number := 211, text := "2. `NNNN` is a four digit decimal prefix, padded on the left with zeros (for example `0000`, `0001`)." },
  { number := 212, text := "3. `0000` is reserved for this document (the Surmount spec spec)." },
  { number := 213, text := "4. `descriptive_name` SHALL use ASCII letters, digits, and underscores only. Filenames that use hyphens in the basename SHALL NOT be used for specs." },
  { number := 214, text := "5. Numbered specification files SHALL live only in the repository root. Specs SHALL NOT be placed under nested directories (for example `specs/`, `docs/specs/`, or similar). See Clause 1.1." },
  { number := 215, text := "6. Once a specification number has been published in the repository, that number SHALL NOT be reused for a different document. Do not renumber published specs." },
  { number := 216, text := "" },
  { number := 217, text := "### 11.2 Citation of other Surmount specs" },
  { number := 218, text := "" },
  { number := 219, text := "1. Other specifications in this repository SHOULD be cited by file name and title, and MAY include a version string (for example `0001_DOGE.md`, Digital Octal Graphical Emissive (DOGE) Color Specification, 1.0.0)." },
  { number := 220, text := "2. When citing a specification that has not been published yet, do not invent a shipped version number for work the operator has not declared published." },
  { number := 221, text := "" },
  { number := 222, text := "### 11.3 Citation of external standards" },
  { number := 223, text := "" },
  { number := 224, text := "1. External standards SHOULD be cited with enough identity for a reader to find them: title, issuing body when known, and version or year when relevant." },
  { number := 225, text := "2. When this repository keeps a local copy under `ref/`, the citation SHOULD also name that path (for example `ref/semver`, `ref/rfc2119`)." },
  { number := 226, text := "" },
  { number := 227, text := "### 11.4 Markdown path links in this repository" },
  { number := 228, text := "" },
  { number := 229, text := "1. Markdown links that reference any material present in this repository SHALL resolve to a path that exists. That includes relative links, paths under `ref/`, sibling `.lean` files next to numbered specifications, and other numbered specification files." },
  { number := 230, text := "2. Lean SHALL check that rule so the link graph among those path targets is consistent with files that exist." },
  { number := 231, text := "3. The check SHALL scan numbered specification files matching `NNNN_descriptive_name.md` at the repository root for markdown links of the form `[text](target)`. It SHOULD also scan `README.md`." },
  { number := 232, text := "4. The check SHALL skip targets that begin with `http://` or `https://`." },
  { number := 233, text := "5. Remaining targets SHALL be resolved relative to the file that contains the link, or relative to the repository root. The check SHALL fail when the resolved path does not exist." },
  { number := 234, text := "6. A target that is only a fragment (`#heading`) is the same file and SHALL NOT fail for a missing path. A target that combines a path and a fragment SHALL be checked on the path before the fragment." },
  { number := 235, text := "7. This floor does not parse every HTML `href`. It does not parse reference style markdown links." },
  { number := 236, text := "" },
  { number := 237, text := "## 12. External references (`ref/`)" },
  { number := 238, text := "" },
  { number := 239, text := "### 12.1 Purpose" },
  { number := 240, text := "" },
  { number := 241, text := "1. `ref/` holds the external standards this repository cites (Semantic Versioning, RFC 2119, ECMA and ISO terminal standards, and similar) so readers and tools can open the **exact** text meant without hunting the web." },
  { number := 242, text := "2. Hyperlinks to publisher pages still matter (and often predate git and even some of the specs). Canonical URLs SHALL remain in each Form 2 entry's `SOURCE.md` file and in each Form 1 sibling `.SOURCE` file, and SHOULD appear in citations." },
  { number := 243, text := "3. Local copies under `ref/` freeze what this repository reviewed and checksummed or pinned in git. They do not replace the publisher page as the public home of the standard." },
  { number := 244, text := "" },
  { number := 245, text := "### 12.2 Process rule (binding)" },
  { number := 246, text := "" },
  { number := 247, text := "1. Any external specification this repository cites MUST be present under `ref/` in one of the two forms in this clause." },
  { number := 248, text := "2. **Form 1: Canonical git that the publisher maintains.** When a cited standard has a **canonical git repository maintained by the publisher** (any host: GitHub, GitLab, a host you run yourself, or other; NOT assumed to be GitHub), authors SHALL add it under `ref/` as a git submodule from that publisher git URL and pin a tag or commit. Example: Semantic Versioning under `ref/semver`." },
  { number := 249, text := "3. **Form 2: No canonical git that the publisher maintains.** When the publisher does not maintain a canonical git repository for that standard (common for standards from before git and from the PDF era), or when official git is not the authoritative publication form, Form 2 is the **intended** process (not a fallback failure). Authors SHALL:" },
  { number := 250, text := "	1. Document that fact in `ref/README.md` for the entry (no publisher git; what is used instead)." },
  { number := 251, text := "	2. Keep a **local copy** of the specific official publisher artifact needed under `ref/<short_name>/`, committed **directly in this repository's git tree** as ordinary files (**not** a submodule), obtained from the most canonical official source the publisher provides." },
  { number := 252, text := "	3. Record canonical hyperlink(s), retrieval date, checksum, and edition or version in a pure ASCII `SOURCE.md` file under that directory. Publisher hyperlinks still matter as publisher links; the local copy freezes the reviewed bytes." },
  { number := 253, text := "	4. Note license, copyright, or republication status for the entry in `ref/README.md` (detail MAY remain in `SOURCE.md`)." },
  { number := 254, text := "	5. State reasoning in `ref/README.md`: why direct tree copy instead of submodule (no publisher git; durable local pin of the exact official artifact; hyperlinks remain for the living publisher location; no invented fake git remotes). That form **does** satisfy this clause. Examples: RFC 2119 (RFC Editor plain text under `ref/rfc2119`), ECMA-48 (Ecma International PDF under `ref/ecma48`), and ITU-T T.416 (ITU free PDF under `ref/itu_t416`; identical text also published as ISO/IEC 8613-6)." },
  { number := 255, text := "4. Authors SHALL NOT invent a random third party mirror or community reformat as \"canonical git.\" Prefer honesty about what the publisher actually maintains." },
  { number := 256, text := "5. Authors SHOULD prefer Form 1 when the publisher truly maintains git for that standard, and SHOULD prefer Form 2 when that is what the publisher ships. Standards from before git and from the PDF era (RFC 2119, ECMA-48, ITU-T T.416, and similar) are expected to use Form 2 unless the publisher later maintains real canonical git for that standard." },
  { number := 257, text := "6. Directory names under `ref/` SHOULD be short, stable, and lowercase (examples: `semver`, `rfc2119`, `ecma48`, `itu_t416`)." },
  { number := 258, text := "7. Authors SHALL NOT edit upstream files inside submodules or inside pinned official artifacts. Specs cite a version, tag, commit, or `SOURCE.md` checksum. Update the pin when the cited version must change." },
  { number := 259, text := "8. Process detail for operators and agents, including per-entry license notes and \"Why we pin this way,\" is described in `ref/README.md`. A short summary also appears in the repository root `README.md`." },
  { number := 260, text := "" },
  { number := 261, text := "### 12.3 Present initial references" },
  { number := 262, text := "" },
  { number := 263, text := "The following are expected early `ref/` entries for this repository when populated. Each form below is valid under Clause 12.2:" },
  { number := 264, text := "" },
  { number := 265, text := "1. Semantic Versioning (`ref/semver`) - Form 1 git submodule of the SemVer project's canonical repository that the publisher maintains, when present." },
  { number := 266, text := "2. RFC 2119 (`ref/rfc2119`) - Form 2 official RFC Editor plain text pin committed directly in this repository's git tree with `SOURCE.md` when present. There is no canonical git repository that the publisher maintains for this historic RFC that this process treats as authoritative; third party copies are not canonical." },
  { number := 267, text := "3. ECMA-48 and related ISO/IEC terminal control references as used by DOGE and similar specs, when available under `ref/` - Form 2 official Ecma International PDF pin committed directly in this repository's git tree with `SOURCE.md` for ECMA-48 (`ref/ecma48`) when present. Community reformats are not the Ecma publisher source." },
  { number := 268, text := "4. ITU-T T.416 / ISO/IEC 8613-6 (`ref/itu_t416`) - Form 2 official ITU free PDF pin (Recommendation T.416, 03/93; identical text also published as ISO/IEC 8613-6) committed directly in this repository's git tree with `SOURCE.md` when present. There is no canonical git that the publisher maintains for this Recommendation. DOGE cites this standard for extended color models." },
  { number := 269, text := "" },
  { number := 270, text := "Population of `ref/` may be performed by a separate effort. Specs MAY be written citing these paths before the directories exist; the paths remain the documented local locations." },
  { number := 271, text := "" },
  { number := 272, text := "### 12.4 Adding or updating a citation" },
  { number := 273, text := "" },
  { number := 274, text := "When a specification gains a new normative or strongly recommended external citation, authors SHALL:" },
  { number := 275, text := "" },
  { number := 276, text := "1. Add or update the corresponding `ref/` entry under Clause 12.2: either a Form 1 git submodule from the standard's canonical git source that the publisher maintains, or a Form 2 official publisher artifact pin with `SOURCE.md` committed directly in this repository's git tree, as fits what the publisher actually provides." },
  { number := 277, text := "2. Cite both the public identity of the standard (including publisher hyperlink when useful) and the local `ref/` path when the path exists." },
  { number := 278, text := "3. Avoid treating an empty or not yet populated `ref/` path as a reason to drop the public citation. Document the intended path and complete the pin when the process allows." },
  { number := 279, text := "4. Authors SHALL NOT delete a valid pin solely to force a different form without operator direction." },
  { number := 280, text := "5. Existing pins remain. New pins are added when the cited artifact is present under `ref/`." },
  { number := 281, text := "6. A Form 2 PDF pin SHALL include `SOURCE.md`. A permission note for republication of those PDFs SHALL be recorded when the operator writes that note. Until that note exists, residual SHALL name that it is not yet written." },
  { number := 282, text := "" },
  { number := 283, text := "## 13. Versioning (Semantic Versioning and publication)" },
  { number := 284, text := "" },
  { number := 285, text := "### 13.1 SemVer is real Semantic Versioning" },
  { number := 286, text := "" },
  { number := 287, text := "1. Specification version numbers SHALL follow Semantic Versioning 2.0.0: MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH." },
  { number := 288, text := "2. Specs SHALL cite SemVer (and `ref/semver` when present)." },
  { number := 289, text := "3. A change that makes previously conforming implementations nonconforming SHALL raise MAJOR and MAY reset MINOR and PATCH to 0 as SemVer describes." },
  { number := 290, text := "4. A backward compatible addition or clarification SHOULD raise MINOR (and MAY reset PATCH to 0) as SemVer describes." },
  { number := 291, text := "5. A backward compatible fix that does not add feature surface SHOULD raise PATCH only." },
  { number := 292, text := "" },
  { number := 293, text := "### 13.2 First publication and honesty about history" },
  { number := 294, text := "" },
  { number := 295, text := "1. The repository operator controls when a specification is published." },
  { number := 296, text := "2. Specs SHALL NOT invent pretend shipped releases for work that was never published." },
  { number := 297, text := "3. By operator order dated 20260831, numbered specifications other than `0001_DOGE.md` carry version line 0.1.0. First publication SHALL NOT be required to be 1.0.0 for those files. Digital Octal Graphical Emissive (DOGE) remains 1.0.0." },
  { number := 298, text := "4. Specs MAY carry a SemVer version line (Clause 5.1) without special draft status wording. Agents and authors SHALL NOT invent fake version history or claim publication the operator has not declared." },
  { number := 299, text := "5. Changelog entries SHOULD use SemVer version headings when versions are assigned. They SHALL NOT label snapshots as shipped version numbers that were never released. Changelog history MAY record that a 1.0.0 line existed." },
  { number := 300, text := "" },
  { number := 301, text := "### 13.3 MAJOR 0" },
  { number := 302, text := "" },
  { number := 303, text := "The current version line 0.1.0 on numbered specifications other than `0001_DOGE.md` is operator order, not a fake 0.x release train. Digital Octal Graphical Emissive (DOGE) remains 1.0.0. Changelog history MAY record that a 1.0.0 or later line existed." },
  { number := 304, text := "" },
  { number := 305, text := "## 14. Required Conformance clause" },
  { number := 306, text := "" },
  { number := 307, text := "This clause states what every normative Surmount specification is required to include inside its own document. It is not the checklist for whether a document meets this document (the form rules in 0000); that checklist is Clause 19." },
  { number := 308, text := "" },
  { number := 309, text := "1. Every normative specification SHALL include a section that states how an implementation or document conforms to that specification (what has to be true to claim conformance)." },
  { number := 310, text := "2. That section SHOULD be titled in plain English so readers can find it (for example \"Conformance\" or \"Conformance requirements\")." },
  { number := 311, text := "3. Documents that are informative only SHALL say they are informative only near the top of the file and need not define implementation conformance." },
  { number := 312, text := "4. Authors SHOULD NOT use the same heading title for (a) the conformance requirements inside that specification for implementers of that spec and (b) any checklist about conforming to this document (the form rules in 0000), when both appear. Prefer distinct titles such as \"Conformance\" for (a) and leave form rule checklists to this document (Clause 19)." },
  { number := 313, text := "" },
  { number := 314, text := "## 15. Change control" },
  { number := 315, text := "" },
  { number := 316, text := "1. After first publication, every normative specification SHALL include a Changelog section." },
  { number := 317, text := "2. Each published revision SHALL add a changelog entry headed with the SemVer version and date form from Clause 5.2 (for example `### 1.1.0 - 20260815`)." },
  { number := 318, text := "3. Changelog entries SHOULD summarize normative changes in plain language. Informative edits MAY be noted briefly or omitted when trivial." },
  { number := 319, text := "4. Version numbering follows Clause 13." },
  { number := 320, text := "5. Specs SHOULD keep a short changelog with SemVer headings once versions are assigned, without fake release numbers for work that was never shipped." },
  { number := 321, text := "" },
  { number := 322, text := "## 16. Copyright and license" },
  { number := 323, text := "" },
  { number := 324, text := "1. Every specification SHALL include a Copyright or License section, or an explicit statement that the repository license applies to the document." },
  { number := 325, text := "2. Unless a specification states otherwise, the repository Unlicense (public domain dedication) applies." },
  { number := 326, text := "3. License sections MAY quote or summarize the Unlicense. They SHALL NOT contradict the repository license without an explicit, visible alternate terms statement." },
  { number := 327, text := "" },
  { number := 328, text := "## 17. Machine validation" },
  { number := 329, text := "" },
  { number := 330, text := "### 17.1 Authoritative checkers" },
  { number := 331, text := "" },
  { number := 332, text := "1. The authoritative automated checker for mechanical form rules on numbered specifications implied by this document is a Lean program, packaged and run through the repository Nix flake as the sole software package (`packages.spec_check`), with a matching flake check, development shell entry, and pre-commit hook." },
  { number := 333, text := "2. Mechanical form rules checked by Lean on numbered specifications include character set, list markers, excess hyphenated tokens in prose (lowercase token with two or more hyphens), leftover unstack phrases listed in `0005_CATE.md`, a Residual heading when a numbered specification other than this document contains a planned or unpublished `NNNN_*.md` token, trailing whitespace, leading indentation that uses tabs only, detection of prose wrapped to a column, and related form rules, and MAY grow as the Lean checker covers more clauses." },
  { number := 334, text := "3. Trailing whitespace and leading indentation that uses tabs only, on Surmount authored text files across the project (not only numbered specifications), SHALL be enforced by a pure Nix flake check (Nix language evaluation over the tree; see `nix/whitespace.nix` and `checks.whitespace`). That check is not bash and not Lean. It skips vendor blobs under `ref/` such as the SemVer submodule, PDFs, and RFC plain text, and applies the Lean/YAML indent exceptions in Clause 6.5." },
  { number := 335, text := "4. Dual whitespace coverage is intentional and both checks SHALL remain: Lean enforces trailing whitespace and leading indentation that uses tabs only on numbered specifications as part of the form suite (item 2); pure Nix enforces the same class of rules across the tree on Surmount authored text (item 3). Neither layer replaces the other. Design rationale for that split (and for keeping spelling and form policy that takes more than one pass in Lean rather than pure Nix) is informative prose in `README.md` section 7.2.1 and in `AGENTS.md` under \"Why Lean vs pure Nix\"; those documents are not additional normative checkers." },
  { number := 336, text := "5. American English spelling against the repository personal word list SHALL be enforced by the Lean `spec_check` program, which invokes `aspell` from PATH (`list --lang=en_US --mode=markdown`) with `aspell_words.txt`. The flake check `checks.spec_check` supplies `pkgs.aspellWithDicts` on PATH so spelling runs in CI. Spelling is not a separate flake check, not a shell package, and MUST NOT rely on a repository `scripts/` gatekeeper or a bash discovery policy that spans multiple lines." },
  { number := 337, text := "6. Documents in scope SHALL remain checkable by the relevant authoritative checker when it is available for a given clause." },
  { number := 338, text := "7. Markdown path links in numbered specifications (Clause 11.4) SHALL be checked by a Lean program packaged through the repository Nix flake (`packages.repo_links_check` and `checks.repo_links_check`). That program is not a second form suite for ASCII, spelling, or leftover unstack. It does not parse every HTML `href`." },
  { number := 339, text := "" },
  { number := 340, text := "### 17.2 Integration points (no bash authority)" },
  { number := 341, text := "" },
  { number := 342, text := "1. Validation entry points are the repository Nix flake: `nix flake check`, `nix build` / `nix run` of `spec_check`, development shell, and pre-commit hook. There is no lasting `scripts/` bash authority for form rules, spelling, or whitespace across the project." },
  { number := 343, text := "2. The Lean program is authoritative for any form rule on numbered specifications that it implements, including American English spelling when aspell is available (Clause 17.1 item 5). Bash MUST NOT be the authority for trailing whitespace or tab indent across the project; that authority is pure Nix (Clause 17.1 item 3)." },
  { number := 344, text := "3. This clause does not encode third party hook product brand names as document law. Repository Nix flake checks and a pre-commit hook are the intended integration points described for humans in `README.md`." },
  { number := 345, text := "" },
  { number := 346, text := "### 17.3 What validation does not decide" },
  { number := 347, text := "" },
  { number := 348, text := "Machine validation does not by itself declare publication, SemVer intent, or correctness of technical algorithms beyond the mechanical form rules it implements." },
  { number := 349, text := "" },
  { number := 350, text := "## 18. Self application" },
  { number := 351, text := "" },
  { number := 352, text := "This document SHALL conform to its own rules." },
  { number := 353, text := "" },
  { number := 354, text := "## 19. Conformance to this document" },
  { number := 355, text := "" },
  { number := 356, text := "A markdown document is conformant to this document if and only if all of the following hold when the clause applies to that document:" },
  { number := 357, text := "" },
  { number := 358, text := "1. The file is pure ASCII as defined in Clause 6.1." },
  { number := 359, text := "2. Prose uses American English as in Clause 6.2 and remains validator checkable as in Clauses 6.3 and 17." },
  { number := 360, text := "3. Line wrapping, tab indentation, and trailing whitespace follow Clauses 6.4, 6.5, and 6.6." },
  { number := 361, text := "4. Section headings and lists follow Clauses 5.3 and 5.4." },
  { number := 362, text := "5. RFC 2119 keywords are used as required by Clause 4 when the document is normative and states requirements." },
  { number := 363, text := "6. Excess hyphenated tokens in prose are absent as required by Clause 7." },
  { number := 364, text := "7. Precision, open questions, and labels for behavior the implementation defines follow Clauses 4.2 and 8." },
  { number := 365, text := "8. If the document is a numbered specification file, naming follows Clause 11." },
  { number := 366, text := "9. External citations follow Clauses 11.3 and 12 when such citations appear." },
  { number := 367, text := "10. Versioning and publication rules follow Clause 13 and Clause 5.2." },
  { number := 368, text := "11. If the document is normative (not a document that is informative only), it includes a Required Conformance clause as in Clause 14, and after first publication it includes a Changelog as in Clause 15." },
  { number := 369, text := "12. Copyright or license material follows Clause 16." },
  { number := 370, text := "13. No binary images are embedded (Clause 10)." },
  { number := 371, text := "14. Leftover unstack phrases listed in `0005_CATE.md` are absent from numbered specification prose outside fenced code and inline code." },
  { number := 372, text := "15. If the document is a numbered specification other than this document and it contains a planned or unpublished `NNNN_*.md` token, it includes a Residual heading as in `0005_CATE.md`." },
  { number := 373, text := "16. CATE requirements in `0005_CATE.md` that are not fully mechanical still apply to authors (complete thoughts, residual written in full, spec markdown as the contract humans read, no invented conforming product, RFC 2119 not replaced)." },
  { number := 374, text := "17. If the document is a numbered specification file, markdown path links that reference material present in this repository resolve to a path that exists as in Clause 11.4." },
  { number := 375, text := "" },
  { number := 376, text := "Optional recommendations (SHOULD, RECOMMENDED) do not break conformance when unmet, but authors SHOULD follow them unless there is a clear reason not to." },
  { number := 377, text := "" },
  { number := 378, text := "## 20. Concise American Technical English (CATE)" },
  { number := 379, text := "" },
  { number := 380, text := "Concise American Technical English is specified in `0005_CATE.md`. This document does not restate CATE. Authors SHALL follow that specification when writing numbered specifications in this repository." },
  { number := 381, text := "" },
  { number := 382, text := "## 21. Changelog" },
  { number := 383, text := "" },
  { number := 384, text := "### 0.1.0 - 20260831" },
  { number := 385, text := "" },
  { number := 386, text := "Current version line is 0.1.0 by operator order. Changelog history records that a 1.4.0 line existed. Numbered specifications other than `0001_DOGE.md` carry version line 0.1.0. Digital Octal Graphical Emissive (DOGE) remains 1.0.0. First publication is not required to be 1.0.0 for those files." },
  { number := 387, text := "" },
  { number := 388, text := "### 1.4.0 - 20260826" },
  { number := 389, text := "" },
  { number := 390, text := "Adds Clause 11.4: markdown links that reference material present in this repository SHALL resolve to a path that exists, and Lean SHALL check that those path targets exist. The floor is markdown `[text](target)` links in numbered specifications and in `README.md`. Targets that begin with `http://` or `https://` are skipped. Fragment only targets are the same file. This floor does not parse every HTML `href` and does not parse reference style links. Clause 17.1 names the Lean program `repo_links_check`. Conformance to this document includes that path link rule. Surmount Systems Tech Tree is numbered specification 0011 (`0011_Surmount_Systems_Tech_Tree.md`)." },
  { number := 391, text := "" },
  { number := 392, text := "### 1.3.0 - 20260825" },
  { number := 393, text := "" },
  { number := 394, text := "Assigns Concise American Technical English to specification 0005 with file name `0005_CATE.md` and Recursive Self Improvement to specification 0006 with file name `0006_RSI.md`. Existing `ref/` pins remain; new pins are added when the cited artifact is present. A PDF permission note is named in residual until the operator writes that note. Lean 4, CompCert, Idris 2, and Rust stay in the Systems Lean product tree and MUST NOT be copied into this repository's `ref/`. Form 2 pin notes are a pure ASCII `SOURCE.md` file, not a bare SOURCE file. Form 1 sibling pin notes remain `ref/semver.SOURCE`." },
  { number := 395, text := "" },
  { number := 396, text := "### 1.2.0 - 20260825" },
  { number := 397, text := "" },
  { number := 398, text := "Moves Concise American Technical English (CATE) out of this document into numbered specification `0005_CATE.md` (first publication of CATE as a numbered specification, version 1.0.0; the stack number 0005 is recorded in 1.3.0). This document keeps a short pointer only and does not restate CATE. Authors still SHALL apply CATE by following `0005_CATE.md`. fargo has no assigned specification number. The Carbonado Network Protocol still has no assigned number." },
  { number := 399, text := "" },
  { number := 400, text := "### 1.1.0 - 20260825" },
  { number := 401, text := "" },
  { number := 402, text := "Adds Concise American Technical English (CATE) as an extension of this document (specification 0000). Recursive Self Improvement is specified in `0006_RSI.md`. fargo has no assigned specification number. CATE states complete American English thoughts, concise prose, leftover unstack as nonconforming, residual written in full, spec markdown as the contract humans read, no invented conforming product, and that CATE does not replace RFC 2119. ISO 24495-1:2023 Plain language and the US Federal Plain Language Guidelines are named as informative prior art and are not Clause 12 pins. Lean `spec_check` flags the leftover unstack floor list and requires a Residual heading when numbered specifications other than this document contain a planned or unpublished `NNNN_*.md` token. Companion meaning encodings live under `lean/CateSpec.lean`. Proofs live under `lean/CateProofs.lean`." },
  { number := 403, text := "" },
  { number := 404, text := "### 1.0.0 - 20260730" },
  { number := 405, text := "" },
  { number := 406, text := "Establishes normative clauses for scope, RFC 2119 requirement keywords, Semantic Versioning with no fake 0.x release history, external references under `ref/`, document skeleton, ASCII and American English rules, hyphenation, spelling checkability, tables and code, file naming and flat repository root location only, Required Conformance clause (what each normative spec must include), Conformance to this document (checklist for meeting 0000), change control, license, Lean based machine validation via Nix, and self application. Clarifies normative versus informative text under RFC 2119 (background and design reasoning are informative; requirements use RFC 2119 keywords; informative sections should be labeled as such). Names this document the Surmount spec spec (spec 0); form rules use plain \"spec spec\" language rather than a hyphenated product name." },
  { number := 407, text := "" },
  { number := 408, text := "Clause 12 process: external standards this repository cites MUST live under `ref/` either as Form 1 git submodules from a canonical git source that the publisher maintains (example: SemVer), or as Form 2 official publisher artifact pins when there is no canonical git that the publisher maintains. Form 2 is the intentional process for those standards (local copy of the official artifact under `ref/`, SOURCE, publisher hyperlinks that still matter), not a fallback failure. Form 2 pins are ordinary files committed directly in this repository's git tree (not submodules), with SOURCE metadata (canonical URL, retrieval date, checksum, edition), license or copyright notes, and reasoning in `ref/README.md`. Examples: RFC 2119 RFC Editor txt, ECMA-48 Ecma PDF, ITU-T T.416 ITU free PDF (dual text ISO/IEC 8613-6) under `ref/itu_t416`. Hyperlinks to publisher pages still matter as publisher links. Third party mirrors and community reformats are not canonical. Official artifact pins for RFC 2119, ECMA-48, and ITU-T T.416 meet the rule; they are not \"legacy gaps.\"" },
  { number := 409, text := "" },
  { number := 410, text := "Whitespace and layout (same 1.0.0 publication line; not a separate shipped release): Clause 6.4 requires prose that is not wrapped to a column (one physical line per paragraph or list item body; soft wrap left to the reader). Clause 6.5 requires leading indentation that uses tabs only in specs (spaces in the middle of a line and in tables remain allowed; Lean and YAML tooling files are documented exceptions for indent with spaces). Clause 6.6 forbids trailing whitespace. Lean `spec_check` enforces trailing whitespace, leading spaces, and a heuristic for wrapping to a column on numbered specifications. Trailing whitespace and tab indent on Surmount authored text across the project are enforced by pure Nix (`checks.whitespace` / `nix/whitespace.nix`), not by bash and not by moving that scan into Lean. Dual whitespace coverage (Lean on numbered specifications plus pure Nix across the tree) is intentional; Clause 17.1 states both and points to `README.md` section 7.2.1 and `AGENTS.md` \"Why Lean vs pure Nix\" for design rationale. American English spelling on numbered specifications is enforced by Lean `spec_check` via aspell and `aspell_words.txt` (flake `checks.spec_check` puts aspellWithDicts on PATH); there is no separate `checks.spelling` and no `scripts/` bash gatekeeper." },
  { number := 411, text := "" },
  { number := 412, text := "This unpublished 1.0.0 also records that hyphenated modifiers in prose were rephrased into ordinary English. Deleting the hyphen and leaving the same two words is not enough. Hyphens remain for filenames, URLs, code identifiers, and official designations." },
  { number := 413, text := "" },
  { number := 414, text := "Prior short checklists and intermediate text are superseded by this version. They were never separate published releases." },
  { number := 415, text := "" },
  { number := 416, text := "## 22. Copyright" },
  { number := 417, text := "" },
  { number := 418, text := "This work is released into the public domain under the Unlicense. Anyone is free to copy, modify, publish, use, compile, sell, or distribute this specification, either in source code form or as a compiled binary, for any purpose, commercial or noncommercial, and by any means. See `UNLICENSE.md` in this repository." },
]

